Z||000008||kslish||07-24-2001||07:20 PM||||Marlin,<P>I sent you some pics as requested of PIP in action.<P>I'm work part time as a Retail Power Rep for Apple.  Mostly I just go and review the performance of the local Western PA Circuit City's and CompUSA's.  I'm responsible for training the stores staff on new products and making sure the demo computers are in good working order.  I also do store demonstrations when new products come out.  Pretty easy way to earn some extra cash.<P>Pittsburgh is also going to be the site of an Apple Store so I may end up there if I can't work my way into Apple's Educational sales force or get a teaching job this fall (trying to get back into teaching even though I have been in the wireless communications field for the past three years until the economy started to go south).  <P>-----<P>Jerry, <P>Yea I think my 6400 won't ever run OS X.  It won't support more than 128MB of memory and it's Open Firmware implementation is pretty poor.  It's 2 PCI slots are already filled with a USB card and the ATI video card.  I've finally outgrown it even though it's light years ahead of the Quadra I originally bought when I was in college.  I just bought a stripped down 9500 for a little over $100 and I plan on swapping all the drives, memory and cards from the 6400 into the 9500.  Unfortunately, I still have to get a G3 upgrade (gonna get a XLR8 ZIFF Carrier card) since the 6400's wont work.  <P>What video card are you running and what speed is your G3 upgrade?  OS X's speed is really dependent on the quality of the graphics card due to the Aqua interface.  Also, OS X's graphics card drivers still haven't been brought up to par unless you have an Nvidia card or an ATI Rage 128 or Radeon (even the Radeon is still a little flaky).  Until recently, Apple was most concerned with the drivers for their currently shipping hardware.  The ATI Rage Pro drivers are really slow and are not optimized yet (use OS X on an old iMac or original iBook and your 8500 may not seem so slow anymore).  I "hear" that by September, drivers for the older graphics chips should be finished.  <P>I'm in no rush to make the switch to OS X, just need a way to run it so I can become more familiar with troubleshooting it.  I just installed the latest build of Mac OS 9.2 on my 6400 (yes, 9.2 does exist but it will probably only be released to the public as part of the 10.1 update to OS X coming in September).  It's mainly a bug fix to 9.1, uses a slightly smaller memory footprint, and it boots a little quicker (mostly improvements to get the OS X Classic compatablity layer running a lot better). <P>Ken S.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Hym-The-Man||07-25-2001||05:53 AM||||Great idea. I want to post pics all of the time, and I don't want to boot up VPC. If you couuld get that going you would be "Da Man".<P>I am sitting here now in fromt of my beige 9600 running 9.1, Beige G3 running OSX 10.0.4 and a 7600/132 running linux. I am a mac nut.<BR> <BR>i used to work at an Apple Higher ED retailer.  The most fun job I ever  had.<P>Good luck and let us know about the software ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Yellow88GT||07-26-2001||05:14 PM||||Ken,<P>Sorry it to so long to get back.<P>I am running an old XLR8 MACh Speed G3 card.<BR>I am running the CPU at 300 MHz and the front side bus at 43 MHz.<P>The video is whatever they built into the motherboard of the 8500, an old ATI?<P>Marlin,<P>It would be great if I could post images with the Mac. It has my scanner, and right now I<BR>have to scan and then copy the images to my Linux box on the network using AppleTalk.<BR>I then post it from my windows PC via samba from the Linux box. <P>What a pain!<P>So if Cliff gives the ok, I think it would be a great project.<P>Would you write a version for OS 9.x or OS X?<P>Thanks,<P>Jerry<p>[This message has been edited by Yellow88GT (edited 07-27-2001).]||||reg||1
